To force a text field in Adobe Flash (now Adobe Animate) to display text in columns, you can use ActionScript, which is the programming language used within Adobe Flash. Here's a simple example of how you might do this:

1. Open your Adobe Flash project and select the TextField you want to format.
2. In the Properties panel, set the `multiline` property to `true` to allow the text field to display multiple lines.
3. Set the `wordWrap` property to `true` to ensure that the text wraps to the next line when it reaches the end of the text field.
4. To create columns, you can use the `setTextFormat` method to apply a text format with a specific `leftMargin` and `rightMargin` to create the illusion of columns.

Here's an example of ActionScript code that you can use to set the text format for a text field to create columns:

```actionscript
// Assuming your text field has an instance name of "myTextField"
var myTextFormat:TextFormat = new TextFormat();
myTextFormat.leftMargin = 20; // Set the left margin to create the first column
myTextFormat.rightMargin = 20; // Set the right margin to create the second column
myTextFormat.font = "Arial"; // Set the font
myTextFormat.size = 12; // Set the font size

myTextField.setTextFormat(myTextFormat);
```

Remember to replace `"myTextField"` with the actual instance name of your text field. Also, adjust the `leftMargin` and `rightMargin` values to create the desired column width.

If you want to dynamically add text to the text field and maintain the column format, you can use the following code:

```actionscript
// Function to add text to the text field in columns
function addTextInColumns(text:String):void {
    var lines:Array = text.split("\n"); // Split the text into lines
    var currentY:Number = 0; // Initialize the Y position

    for (var i:int = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        myTextField.appendText(lines[i] + "\n"); // Append each line to the text field
        currentY += myTextField.getLineHeight(); // Move the Y position down by the line height
    }
}

// Example usage
addTextInColumns("This is line 1 of text\nThis is line 2 of text\nThis is line 3 of text");
```

This function splits the input text into lines and appends each line to the text field, adjusting the Y position to create the effect of columns. You can call this function whenever you need to add text to the text field in columns.

Please note that Adobe Flash and ActionScript are considered legacy technologies, and Adobe has discontinued their support. For new projects, consider using modern web technologies like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

